# Cursor rules for <PROJECT_NAME>
#
# Cursor reads this file at the start of every session in this repo. Treat it
# as a thin pointer at the canonical sources of truth, not as another place
# to keep architecture documentation. Anything that contradicts CLAUDE.md is
# wrong — CLAUDE.md wins.

# === Source of truth ===

The authoritative architecture document is `CLAUDE.md` at the repo root.
Read it before proposing structural changes. The README is for users; the
CLAUDE.md is for AI tools and contributors making code changes. When the
two disagree, CLAUDE.md wins and the README is updated to match.

# === Behavior preservation (non-negotiable) ===

This repo follows `repo-standards` PROMPT.md rule 2:

  1. Keep 100% of original functionality. Never bundle a behavior change
     into a refactor.
  2. Analyze the target code paths *before* editing. Form a mental model
     of the existing observable behavior first.
  3. Every PR description and post-task self-check includes a
     "Repo-specific risks / edge-cases" subsection. "None observed" is an
     acceptable value; the heading must be present.

If the project ships a service worker, manifest.json, or any client-side
storage (localStorage, IndexedDB, caches), follow the PWA Refactor Addendum
in `REFACTORING_GUIDE.md` from repo-standards before touching any of it.

# === Conventional Commits ===

Every commit message follows https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/ .
Allowed types: feat, fix, docs, refactor, test, chore, ci, perf, style,
revert. Optional scope in parens. Breaking changes get `!` and a
`BREAKING CHANGE:` footer. The `commit-msg` pre-commit hook blocks
non-conformant messages locally; CI re-checks the merge commit.

# === Tiny-commit rhythm ===

Default to one file change per commit unless two changes are atomically
inseparable (rare). Each commit is preceded by a one-paragraph plain-English
plan, followed by a self-check (files exist / YAML+JSON parses / links
resolve / line-count delta matches the plan / no template placeholders
leaked outside `templates/`), then a one-line summary. This matches how
`repo-standards` PROMPT.md rule 9 expects upgrades to be performed.

# === Where things live ===

- `CLAUDE.md` — architecture, build commands, conventions, tech stack.
- `README.md` — user-facing docs, getting started, project structure.
- `.github/workflows/` — CI/CD; every workflow has `permissions:`,
  40-char SHA pins, and `timeout-minutes`.
- `.github/dependabot.yml` — weekly dependency updates.
- `templates/` — only present in the repo-standards repo itself; ignore
  here unless this *is* the standards repo.

# === Working alongside other AI tools ===

If contributors also use Claude Code, GitHub Copilot, or Codex on this
repo, see `ai/AI_TEAM_PLAYBOOK.md` for the conventions that keep multiple
AI tools from stepping on each other.
